## 5. Formal Definitions of Epistemic Progress

### 5.1 True Epistemic Progress

Let $P_{\mathrm{world}}(x, y)$ be the true distribution: $x \sim \mu$ from a fixed test measure, $y = h^*(x)$.

**Definition 5.1 (True Epistemic Progress).**

$$\Delta_t = \mathbb{E}_{(x,y) \sim P_{\mathrm{world}}} \left[ \log P_{t+1}(y \mid x) - \log P_t(y \mid x) \right].$$

This definition is strictly non-circular. The test distribution $P_{\mathrm{world}}$ is fixed by the world; evaluation data is drawn independently of the agent's queries; no self-prediction can inflate this quantity by design.

**Proposition 5.1 (True Progress as KL Divergence).**

$$\Delta_t = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_t) - \mathbb{E}_x\left[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}}(\cdot|x) \| P_{t+1}(\cdot|x))\right].$$

*Proof.* Expand:

$$\Delta_t = \mathbb{E}_{x,y}\!\left[\log \frac{P_{t+1}(y|x)}{P_t(y|x)}\right] = \mathbb{E}_{x,y}\!\left[\log \frac{P_{\mathrm{world}}(y|x)}{P_t(y|x)} - \log \frac{P_{\mathrm{world}}(y|x)}{P_{t+1}(y|x)}\right] = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_t) - \mathbb{E}_x[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_{t+1})]. \quad\square$$

**Corollary 5.1 (Bounded Cumulative True Progress).**

$$\sum_{t=1}^T \Delta_t \le D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_0) \le \log H.$$

*Proof.* Telescope Proposition 5.1: $\sum_{t=1}^T \Delta_t = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_0) - \mathbb{E}_x[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_{T+1})] \le D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}} \| P_0) \le \log H$. $\square$

A grounded agent accumulates true progress up to this ceiling. A delusional agent violates it on its self-assessed progress while simultaneously accumulating negative true progress.

### 5.2 Self-Assessed Progress

**Definition 5.2 (Self-Assessed Progress).**

$$\hat{\Delta}_t = \log P_{t+1}(\tilde{y}_t \mid \tilde{x}_t) - \log P_t(\tilde{y}_t \mid \tilde{x}_t),$$

where $P_{t+1}$ incorporates both real and self-generated data via (4.1).

**Proposition 5.2 (Self-Assessed Progress as MAP Mass Shift).** For deterministic hypotheses,

$$\hat{\Delta}_t = \log \frac{P_{t+1}(h_{\mathrm{MAP}}^{(t)})}{P_t(h_{\mathrm{MAP}}^{(t)})}.$$

*Proof.* Let $S_t = \{h : h(\tilde{x}_t) = \tilde{y}_t\}$; the MAP hypothesis belongs to $S_t$. After (4.1), each $h \in S_t$ gains factor $e^\lambda$; others unchanged. In the two-hypothesis construction, $S_t = \{h_2\}$ and $P_t(S_t) = p_t = P_t(h_{\mathrm{MAP}})$, giving the ratio directly. $\square$

The central quantity of study is the divergence $G_T = \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \hat{\Delta}_t - \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \Delta_t$. The Delusion Theorem proves $G_T \to +\infty$.

