## 8. Connections to Related Formal Frameworks

**Self-confirming equilibrium.** The collapse is structurally isomorphic to Fudenberg and Levine's self-confirming equilibrium. The agreement set $\mathcal{A}$ is the equilibrium path; the disagreement set $\mathcal{D}$ is off-path. The critical difference is that self-generated data actively corrupts beliefs on observed points — a more severe failure than merely failing to learn off-path behaviour.

**Reward hacking.** The Delusion Theorem is a Bayesian reward hacking theorem. The proxy $\sum \hat{\Delta}_t$ converges to the finite positive constant $\log 2$ while the true objective $\sum \Delta_t \to -\infty$. The agent simultaneously hacks its own reward and believes the hack. There is no external adversary; the agent is its own deceiver.

**Model collapse.** Model collapse shows progressive loss of distributional fidelity when generative models train on their own outputs. The Delusion Theorem provides the hypothesis-testing analogue: self-generated evidence distorts the posterior exponentially. Both are instances of recursive self-contamination; the present work quantifies the rate ($e^{-\lambda t}$), the threshold ($\lambda > 0^+$), and the phenomenology (bounded apparent progress, unbounded true collapse).

**Impossibility of safe self-evaluation.** Information-theoretic safety gate results show that any gate with bounded false acceptance rate cannot achieve unbounded true positive rate. Theorem 6.3 provides a constructive Bayesian analogue: even with perfect Bayesian updating on real data, any positive self-evaluation weight causes collapse. The grounded framework provides the only structural escape: $\lambda = 0$, i.e., complete externalisation.

