## 10. Empirical Validation Design

Four agents are compared. The **Random** agent queries uniformly and updates via Bayes on real data. The **Oracle EIG Maximiser** selects queries maximising exact EIG with knowledge of $h^*$ — an idealised upper bound. The **Delusional** agent uses the fixed agreement-query, disagreement-self-query strategy from Theorem 6.1 with $\lambda = \log 2$. The **Grounded Meta-Learner** is the proposed framework.

| Agent | $\lim \sum \hat{\Delta}_t$ | $\lim \sum \Delta_t$ | MAP accuracy |
|---|---|---|---|
| Random | — | $\approx \log H$ | $\to 1$ |
| Oracle EIG | — | $\approx \log H$ | $\to 1$ |
| Delusional | $\to \log 2$ | $\to -\infty$ | $\to 0$ |
| Grounded | — | $\approx \log H$ | $\to 1$ |

Boundary experiments vary the self-evaluation weight $\alpha \in [0,1]$ to map the transition from safe to delusional, empirically locating $\alpha_{\mathrm{crit}}$ as a function of the agreement-to-disagreement ratio. Rate experiments vary $\lambda$ and fit exponential decay curves to $q_t$, verifying the predicted rate $e^{-\lambda t}$. Noise experiments add Gaussian noise and track the submartingale $V_t = \log\tfrac{P_t(h_d)}{P_t(h^*)}$, verifying the almost-sure divergence.

