## 11. Future Directions

The core thesis is confined to a finite, deterministic, noiseless symbolic domain to ensure provable results. Several extensions are natural.

**Infinite hypothesis spaces.** Replacing exact Bayes with variational or Monte Carlo inference. The delusion mechanism persists as a property of the fixed points of self-training in function space.

**High-dimensional domains.** Extension to neural network function classes with Laplace approximation or SGMCMC. The grounding principle is architecture-independent.

**Multi-agent collective delusion.** A network of agents exchanging self-generated data may exhibit synchronised collapse — a formal analogue of information cascades — requiring new tools from distributed Bayesian inference.

**Real-world scientific discovery.** Translating the external progress metric to physical measurements and pre-registered predictions. The dissertation provides a formal account of why pre-registration and independent replication are necessary, not merely conventional.

---

## 12. Conclusion

This dissertation has addressed a precise foundational gap: how to measure epistemic progress in a hypothesis-generating system without falling into circular self-evaluation.

The core impossibility result — the Delusion Theorem — establishes that self-evaluation is structurally unsafe. For any finite hypothesis space containing a decoy, and for any positive self-evaluation weight, the agent's self-assessed progress converges to a finite positive constant while its true predictive ability collapses without bound. This is not a failure of sophistication. It can happen to a perfect Bayesian agent. It is a structural consequence of using predictions as evidence about prediction quality.

The core positive result — the Grounded Meta-Learning Theorem — establishes that safe and optimal learning is possible through one architectural principle: strict external evaluation. A meta-learning framework that never allows self-generated signals to influence the reward converges almost surely to a stationary point of the true expected information gain objective. The stationary point is optimal in the sense that it recovers the classical Bayesian experimental design objective under a natural alignment condition. Safety and optimality are not in tension; the external grounding that prevents delusion is precisely what aligns the objective with information gain.

Together these results prove one true thing and prove it completely. The epistemic safety of a self-improving reasoner is not a matter of degree. It is a structural property that depends entirely on whether the evaluation signal has a genuine external ground. Any contamination of that signal by the agent's own predictions leads, inevitably and exponentially, to collapse.

The implications extend beyond the formal framework. A scientific community that validates theories using predictions generated by the same models it is evaluating is structurally vulnerable to exactly the delusional collapse described here. An AI system that uses its own confidence as evidence of its own correctness is running the construction in Theorem 6.1 in continuous time. The remedy in both cases is the same: strict, irreducible grounding in data the evaluating system did not generate and cannot predict in advance.

The work is narrow, deep, and addresses a critical vulnerability of future AI systems. It proves one true thing — and proves it completely.

