Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries and Notation

This appendix collects the foundational definitions and notation used throughout the thesis. All objects are defined in the deterministic, noiseless, finite‑hypothesis setting.

A.1 Hypothesis Space and Environment

Let \mathcal{H} be a finite set of deterministic functions h:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R} with |\mathcal{H}| = H < \infty. The true (unknown) hypothesis is denoted h^*\in\mathcal{H}. The input domain \mathcal{X} may be discrete or continuous; in the symbolic regression testbed it is [-5,5]. At each discrete time t=0,1,2,\dots the agent selects a query point x_t\in\mathcal{X} and receives the noiseless observation y_t = h^*(x_t).

A.2 Belief State and Bayesian Updating

The agent maintains a probability distribution over \mathcal{H}, written P_t\in\Delta(\mathcal{H}), with initial prior P_0 satisfying P_0(h)>0 for all h. After observing a genuine data point (x,y) the posterior is obtained by Bayes’ rule:

P_{t+1}(h) \propto P_t(h) \cdot \mathbf{1}[h(x)=y].

For deterministic hypotheses this update zeros out all inconsistent hypotheses and renormalises the remaining mass.

Lemma A.1 (Posterior Contraction). If h^*\in\mathcal{H} and P_0 has full support, then for any query sequence that visits every point where hypotheses disagree infinitely often, P_t(h^*)\to 1 as t\to\infty.

Proof. Each query at a disagreement point provides positive evidence for h^*. The posterior mass on h^* is a non‑negative bounded martingale with strictly positive expected increments at such points, hence converges almost surely to 1 by the martingale convergence theorem. ∎

A.3 Information‑Theoretic Quantities

For two distributions P,Q on \mathcal{H} the Kullback–Leibler divergence is

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|Q)=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}P(h)\log\frac{P(h)}{Q(h)}.

Given an observation (x,y), the Shannon information gain is

\mathrm{SIG}(x,y)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{t+1}\|P_t)=\log\frac{P_{t+1}(h^*)}{P_t(h^*)}.

Proposition A.2. The expected information gain from querying x equals the expected reduction in posterior entropy:

\mathbb{E}_{y\sim P_t(\cdot|x)}[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{t+1}\|P_t)] = H(P_t) - \mathbb{E}_y[H(P_{t+1})],

where H(P)=-\sum_h P(h)\log P(h). (Proof is standard; see MacKay, 1992.)

A.4 Predictive Distribution

Because every h is deterministic, the agent’s predictive distribution for an input x is simply the sum of posterior mass over all hypotheses that output a specific value:

P_t(y\mid x) = \sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}} P_t(h)\cdot\mathbf{1}[h(x)=y].

The most probable output is \tilde{y}_t = h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)}(x) where h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)} = \arg\max_h P_t(h) (ties broken arbitrarily but consistently).

A.5 Soft Self‑Evaluation Update

A self‑evaluating agent generates a self‑query \tilde{x}_t and a self‑prediction \tilde{y}_t = h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)}(\tilde{x}_t). These are incorporated into the posterior via a pseudo‑likelihood with confidence parameter \lambda>0:

P_{t+1}(h) \propto P_t(h) \cdot \mathbf{1}[h(x_t)=y_t] \cdot \exp\!\bigl(\lambda\cdot\mathbf{1}[h(\tilde{x}_t)=\tilde{y}_t]\bigr). \tag{A.1}

The case \lambda=0 reduces to standard Bayesian updating; any \lambda>0 introduces self‑confirmation.

