Appendix B: The Delusion Theorem – Complete Proofs

B.1 Definitions of Epistemic Progress

Let \mu be a fixed test distribution over \mathcal{X} and let P_{\mathrm{world}} be the joint distribution induced by x\sim\mu and y=h^*(x).

Definition B.1 (True Epistemic Progress).

\Delta_t = \mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim P_{\mathrm{world}}}\bigl[ \log P_{t+1}(y\mid x) - \log P_t(y\mid x) \bigr].

Proposition B.1. \Delta_t = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}}\|P_t) - \mathbb{E}_x[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}}(\cdot|x)\|P_{t+1}(\cdot|x))].
Proof. Expand the log‑ratio and use the definition of KL divergence. ∎

Corollary B.1. \sum_{t=1}^T \Delta_t \le D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{world}}\|P_0) \le \log H. Thus cumulative true progress is bounded.

Definition B.2 (Self‑Assessed Progress).

\hat{\Delta}_t = \log P_{t+1}(\tilde{y}_t\mid\tilde{x}_t) - \log P_t(\tilde{y}_t\mid\tilde{x}_t),

where P_{t+1} is obtained from (A.1).

Proposition B.2 (MAP‑mass shift). For deterministic hypotheses, \hat{\Delta}_t = \log\frac{P_{t+1}(h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)})}{P_t(h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)})}.
Proof. In the two‑hypothesis construction used below the identity is verified directly; the general case follows because the self‑update multiplies the MAP mass by e^\lambda while the rest of the mass on the predicted value receives the same factor, leaving the ratio exactly as stated. ∎

B.2 Safe‑Query Isolation

Lemma B.3 (Safe‑Query Isolation). Let \mathcal{A}_t = \{x : h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)}(x) = h^*(x)\}. If the agent always chooses x_t\in\mathcal{A}_t, then the real observation provides zero relative information about h_{\text{MAP}}^{(t)} versus h^*; i.e., \frac{P_{t}^{\text{real}}(h_{\text{MAP}})}{P_{t}^{\text{real}}(h^*)} is unchanged.
Proof. For any x_t\in\mathcal{A}_t, both hypotheses output the same value, so the likelihood ratio is 1. ∎

B.3 The Two‑Hypothesis Collapse

Theorem B.4 (Delusional Collapse). There exist \mathcal{H}=\{h_1,h_2\}, h^*=h_1, a prior P_0(h_1)=P_0(h_2)=\tfrac12, and a self‑evaluation strategy with \lambda=\log 2 such that, almost surely,

\lim_{T\to\infty}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hat{\Delta}_t = \log 2, \qquad
\lim_{T\to\infty}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\Delta_t = -\infty.

Construction. Choose x_{\mathrm{agr}} where h_1(x_{\mathrm{agr}})=h_2(x_{\mathrm{agr}}) and x_{\mathrm{dis}} where they disagree. The agent’s fixed strategy:

1. Real query at x_{\mathrm{agr}}.
2. Self‑query at x_{\mathrm{dis}}; predict \tilde{y}_t = h_2(x_{\mathrm{dis}}) (tie initially broken for h_2).
3. Update via (A.1) with \lambda=\log 2.

By Lemma B.3 the real query gives zero information, so the update reduces to

P_{t+1}(h) \propto P_t(h)\cdot\exp\!\bigl(\lambda\cdot\mathbf{1}[h(x_{\mathrm{dis}})=\tilde{y}_t]\bigr).

Let p_t = P_t(h_2), q_t = P_t(h_1)=1-p_t. Then

p_{t+1} = \frac{p_t e^\lambda}{p_t e^\lambda + (1-p_t)} = \frac{2p_t}{1+p_t}. \tag{B.2}

Recurrence analysis. The fixed point p=1 is stable (derivative \frac12). Expressing in q_t:

q_{t+1} = \frac{q_t}{2-q_t} \le \frac{q_t}{2},

so q_t \le 2^{-t}q_0 and q_t\to 0, p_t\to 1 exponentially.

Self‑assessed progress. Only h_2 predicts \tilde{y}_t at x_{\mathrm{dis}}, hence P_t(\tilde{y}_t\mid x_{\mathrm{dis}})=p_t. Then

\hat{\Delta}_t = \log\frac{p_{t+1}}{p_t} = \log\frac{2}{1+p_t}.

Telescoping gives \sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hat{\Delta}_t = \log p_T + \log 2 \to \log 2.

True progress. Put test mass at x_{\mathrm{dis}} with true label h_1(x_{\mathrm{dis}}). Then P_t(h_1(x_{\mathrm{dis}})\mid x_{\mathrm{dis}})=q_t, and

\Delta_t = \log q_{t+1} - \log q_t = \log\frac{1}{2-q_t} < -\log 2.

Summing yields \sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\Delta_t = \log q_T + \log 2 \to -\infty.

The gap G_T = \sum(\hat{\Delta}_t - \Delta_t) = \log\frac{p_T}{q_T} \to +\infty. ∎

Remark. Any \lambda>0 yields the same qualitative behaviour; the total self‑assessed progress becomes \log\!\bigl(\frac{e^\lambda}{e^\lambda-1}\bigr), still finite, while true progress diverges to -\infty.

B.4 Generalization to Arbitrary Finite \mathcal{H}

Definition B.5 (Decoy hypothesis). h_d\in\mathcal{H}, h_d\neq h^*, is a decoy if both \mathcal{D}=\{x:h_d(x)\neq h^*(x)\} and \mathcal{A}=\{x:h_d(x)=h^*(x)\} are non‑empty.

Theorem B.6 (Generalized Delusional Collapse). For any finite \mathcal{H} containing a decoy h_d and any \lambda>0, there exists a prior P_0 and a self‑evaluation strategy such that a.s.

\lim_{T\to\infty}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\hat{\Delta}_t = C < \infty,\qquad \lim_{T\to\infty}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\Delta_t = -\infty,

where C = \log\!\bigl(\frac{e^\lambda P_0(h_d)}{1-P_0(h_d)}\bigr).

Proof sketch. Fix x_{\mathrm{agr}}\in\mathcal{A}, x_{\mathrm{dis}}\in\mathcal{D}. The agent always self‑predicts h_d(x_{\mathrm{dis}}). Real queries at x_{\mathrm{agr}} give zero relative information. The self‑update multiplies the mass of all hypotheses that agree with h_d on x_{\mathrm{dis}} by e^\lambda. In particular \frac{P_{t+1}(h_d)}{P_{t+1}(h^*)} = e^\lambda \frac{P_t(h_d)}{P_t(h^*)}, growing exponentially. Hence P_t(h^*)\to 0 at rate e^{-\lambda t}. The true progress therefore decays linearly, and the sum diverges to -\infty. The self‑assessed sum telescopes to the stated constant. ∎

B.5 No‑Free‑Lunch for Self‑Evaluation

Theorem B.7. For any \lambda>0 there exists a finite hypothesis space such that the self‑evaluating agent suffers unbounded true progress collapse while self‑assessed progress converges to a finite constant.

Proof. Apply the general recurrence p_{t+1}=\frac{p_t e^\lambda}{p_t e^\lambda+(1-p_t)} to the two‑hypothesis setup. The fixed point p=1 is stable for all \lambda>0. True progress per step is negative and sum diverges; self‑assessed sum converges to \log\!\bigl(\frac{e^\lambda p_0}{1-p_0}\bigr). ∎

B.6 Extension to Noisy Observations (sketch)

Under Gaussian noise with real variance \sigma_{\mathrm{real}}^2 and smaller self‑variance \sigma_{\mathrm{self}}^2, the pseudo‑likelihood for self‑data becomes \exp\!\bigl(-\frac{(\tilde{y}-h(\tilde{x}))^2}{2\sigma_{\mathrm{self}}^2}\bigr). At a disagreement point the log‑ratio gains a constant positive drift, making the log‑posterior ratio a submartingale that diverges to +\infty. Thus P_t(h^*)\to 0 and true predictive log‑likelihood diverges to -\infty a.s. (Full rigorous proof omitted here.)

