Appendix C: Grounded Meta‑Learning – Formal Theory and Proofs

C.1 Setup and Assumptions

A task is a deterministic function h\in\mathcal{H}, distributed according to \mathcal{T}. The agent uses a stochastic policy \pi_\theta with horizon K. The belief state at step k is s_k = (P_k, k/K), with P_k obtained from real data only. At the end of an episode, the true progress is evaluated on a fixed held‑out test set:

\Delta(\pi_\theta,h) = \mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu}\bigl[ \log P_K(h(x)\mid x) - \log P_0(h(x)\mid x) \bigr],

where \mu is a test distribution independent of the agent’s queries.

The meta‑objective is J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{h\sim\mathcal{T}_{\text{train}}}[\Delta(\pi_\theta,h)].

We adopt the following assumptions:

1. Finite deterministic \mathcal{H}. H<\infty; all hypotheses are deterministic.
2. Smooth bounded policy. \|\nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta(x|s)\|\le G.
3. Bounded true progress. |\Delta|\le R. (For K steps, R=K\log H.)
4. Lipschitz gradient. \nabla J is L-Lipschitz.
5. Robbins–Monro step sizes. \sum\eta_t=\infty, \sum\eta_t^2<\infty.
6. Unbiased gradient estimates. \mathbb{E}[\hat{g}_t\mid\theta_t] = \nabla J(\theta_t).

C.2 Policy Gradient Derivation

Proposition C.1. Under Assumption 2,

\nabla_\theta J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{h\sim\mathcal{T}}\!\left[ \mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi_\theta}\!\Bigl[ \Delta(\pi_\theta,h) \sum_{k=1}^K \nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta(x_k|s_k) \Bigr] \right].

Proof. For a fixed h, the trajectory distribution is \prod_k\pi_\theta(x_k|s_k). The score function estimator gives \nabla_\theta\mathbb{E}_\tau[\Delta] = \mathbb{E}_\tau[\Delta\sum_k\nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta]. Taking expectation over h completes the proof. ∎

In practice we use the Monte Carlo estimate

\hat{g}_t = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^M \Delta_i \sum_{k=1}^K \nabla_\theta\log\pi_{\theta_t}(x_k^{(i)}|s_k^{(i)}).

C.3 Almost‑Sure Convergence

Theorem C.2 (Convergence of Grounded Meta‑Learning). Under the above assumptions, the update \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + \eta_t\hat{g}_t satisfies

\Pr\!\left(\lim_{t\to\infty}\nabla_\theta J(\theta_t)=0\right)=1.

Proof. Write \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + \eta_t(\nabla J(\theta_t) + \xi_t) with \xi_t = \hat{g}_t - \nabla J(\theta_t). By Proposition C.1, \mathbb{E}[\xi_t|\mathcal{F}_t]=0. Moreover \|\xi_t\|\le 2KGR a.s., so \mathbb{E}[\|\xi_t\|^2] is bounded. The drift term \nabla J is Lipschitz. The Robbins–Monro theorem for Lipschitz mappings with square‑integrable martingale‑difference noise (Borkar, 1997, Theorem 2.1) then guarantees convergence of \theta_t to the set \{\theta:\nabla J(\theta)=0\} almost surely, provided the iterates remain bounded. Boundedness can be enforced by projection onto a large compact set without affecting interior stationary points. ∎

Lemma C.3 (Variance bound). \operatorname{Var}(\hat{g}_t)\le (KGR)^2.
Proof. Each trajectory contribution is bounded in norm by KGR; the average has the same maximum norm. ∎

Lemma C.4 (Lipschitz gradient). The gradient of J is Lipschitz. This follows from smoothness of \pi_\theta and boundedness of \Delta; the Hessian involves second derivatives of the log‑policy and products of first derivatives, all bounded on a compact parameter domain.

C.4 Alignment with Bayesian Information Gain

Definition C.5 (Bayesian Information Gain). For an episode with final posterior P_K,

\mathrm{EIG}(\tau) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_K\|P_0).

Theorem C.6 (Alignment of True Progress and EIG). If the test distribution \mu equals the marginal query distribution of \pi_\theta, i.e.

\mu(x) = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^K \mathbb{E}_{\tau}[\mathbf{1}_{x_k=x}],

then

J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{h\sim\mathcal{T}}\,\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi_\theta}\!\bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_K\|P_0) \bigr].

Proof. For any trajectory and deterministic hypotheses, the on‑policy cumulative log‑predictive improvement satisfies

\sum_{k=1}^K\bigl[\log P_k(y_k|x_k)-\log P_{k-1}(y_k|x_k)\bigr] = \log\frac{P_K(h)}{P_0(h)}.

Taking expectation over h and \tau gives \mathbb{E}[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_K\|P_0)]. Because the test points are drawn independently from the same marginal distribution as the queries, the expected true progress over the test set equals the expected on‑policy cumulative improvement. Hence the two objectives coincide. ∎

Thus the grounded meta‑learner not only avoids delusion but also implicitly optimises the classical Bayesian experimental design objective – safe and optimal.

