Appendix D: Architecture and Technical Specifications

D.1 System Components

The grounded agent comprises three modules with strict data‑flow constraints:

· Belief Module: Maintains an exact Bayesian posterior over the enumerated hypothesis space. Updated solely with real oracle observations; self‑generated data never enters.
· Policy Module: A stochastic policy \pi_\theta that maps the belief state (posterior vector concatenated with normalised step index) to a distribution over queries.
· Meta‑Learner: An outer training loop that computes the true progress signal from a held‑out oracle test set and performs policy gradient updates. No self‑predicted labels are used in the evaluation.

D.2 Policy Network Architecture

· Input: H-dimensional posterior probability vector + scalar \frac{k}{K}.
· Hidden layers: Two fully connected layers, 128 units each, ReLU activation.
· Output: Mean \mu and log‑variance \log\sigma^2 of a Gaussian over \mathcal{X}=[-5,5]. The variance is clamped: \sigma \ge 0.1 to ensure exploration.

D.3 Symbolic Regression Testbed

· Grammar: Arithmetic expressions with operations \{+, -,\times,\div,\sin,\cos,\exp,\log\}, constants \{0,1,2,\pi,e\}, variable x. Maximum expression depth: 4.
· Hypothesis enumeration: Exhaustive generation up to depth 4; semantic duplicates removed. Typical size |\mathcal{H}| \approx 10^4 \text{–} 10^5.
· Input domain: \mathcal{X} = [-5,5], continuous for policy sampling; discretised to 1000 points for baseline evaluation.
· Oracle: Noiseless function evaluation.

D.4 Meta‑Training Algorithm (Pseudocode)

```
Input: task distribution T, policy π_θ, horizon K, learning rate η
Initialise θ
for meta-iteration = 1, 2, ... do
    Sample batch of M tasks {h_i} ~ T_train
    for each task h_i do
        P ← uniform prior
        for k = 1 to K do
            s_k ← (P, k/K)
            sample x_k ~ π_θ(· | s_k)
            y_k ← h_i(x_k)                    // real oracle only
            P ← BayesUpdate(P, (x_k, y_k))
        end
        compute Δ_i on held‑out oracle test set
    end
    ĝ ← (1/M) Σ_i Δ_i Σ_k ∇_θ log π_θ(x_k | s_k)
    θ ← θ + η · ĝ
    // optional: evaluate on validation tasks
end
```

D.5 Delusion Demonstration Baseline

The delusional agent is implemented exactly as in the construction of Theorem B.4:

· Fixed real query x_{\mathrm{agr}}=0.
· Self‑query x_{\mathrm{dis}}=1 (chosen such that h_1(1)\neq h_2(1)).
· Soft self‑update with \lambda = \log 2.

D.6 Training Hyperparameters

· Optimiser: Adam, initial learning rate 10^{-3}.
· Learning rate schedule: \eta_t = \eta_0 / (1 + \beta t) with \beta=10^{-4} (approximates Robbins–Monro).
· Mini‑batch size: M=32 tasks per meta‑iteration.
· Episode horizon: K=10 queries per task.
· Variance reduction: Linear value‑function baseline trained on state features (posterior entropy, step index).
· Evaluation: Every 100 meta‑iterations on 100 held‑out validation tasks.
· Seeds: Multiple random seeds for statistical significance.

D.7 Performance Metrics

· Cumulative true progress \sum\Delta_t on held‑out oracle test pairs.
· Cumulative self‑assessed progress \sum\hat{\Delta}_t (delusion baseline only).
· Accuracy of the MAP hypothesis.
· Posterior entropy and analytic EIG.
· Divergence |\sum\hat{\Delta}_t - \sum\Delta_t|.

D.8 Computational Resources

Finite enumeration, exact Bayesian updates, and policy gradient estimation are feasible on a standard CPU workstation. A GPU is optional for faster policy network training. The total compute budget is modest (a few GPU‑days).


